  <h1>Old TV Recycling</h1>
  <p class="lede">Old TV recycling applies to both CRT televisions — which contain leaded glass — and modern flat-panel models with LCD, LED, or OLED screens. Both types must be recycled through authorized facilities under E-Waste Rules 2022 to prevent hazardous material release and recover valuable components.</p>

  <CtaBar whatsappMessage="Hi, I have a question about Old TV Recycling." />

  <ul class="key-takeaways">
    <li>CRT TVs contain leaded glass in the funnel and neck, making them highly hazardous if broken or incinerated.</li>
    <li>Informal TV recycling — including open burning and acid stripping — releases lead, brominated flame retardants, and other toxins into air, soil, and water.</li>
    <li>Authorized recyclers dismantle TVs in controlled environments.</li>
  </ul>

  <section>
    <h2>CRT vs Flat Panel Differences</h2>
    <p>CRT TVs contain leaded glass in the funnel and neck, making them highly hazardous if broken or incinerated. Flat-panel TVs are less hazardous but still contain circuit boards with lead solder, backlight components, and aluminum frames that require controlled recycling.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Hazards of Informal Disposal</h2>
    <p>Informal TV recycling — including open burning and acid stripping — releases lead, brominated flame retardants, and other toxins into air, soil, and water. Communities near informal recycling sites show elevated blood lead levels and respiratory issues.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Authorized Recycling Pathway</h2>
    <p>Authorized recyclers dismantle TVs in controlled environments. CRT glass is processed in lead-safe furnaces. Flat-panel glass, aluminum, copper, and circuit boards are separated and recovered. Plastics are sorted for material recycling.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details>
  <summary>Can I throw an old TV in the trash?</summary>
  <p>No. Both CRT and flat-panel TVs are e-waste under E-Waste Rules 2022 and must be recycled through authorized channels.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>Is CRT TV recycling more expensive?</summary>
  <p>CRT recycling involves additional lead-safe processing but is typically included in standard household pickup programs at no charge.</p>
</details>
  <details>
  <summary>Do you accept old TVs?</summary>
  <p>Yes, both CRT and flat-panel televisions are accepted through our pickup and drop-off programs.</p>
</details>
